# HTTP-only Super E2E deleted-peer convergence flake

## Reproduction before the fix

Command:

```text
go test -race -count=10 . -run 'TestHTTPOnlySuperEndToEnd'
```

Result: 8 failures in 28.635s, all at the deleted-peer convergence assertion.

Focused causal regression before the fix:

```text
go test -race -count=1 . -run 'TestControlHTTPV2SSETerminates_whenHubCloses'
--- FAIL: TestControlHTTPV2SSETerminates_whenHubCloses (1.01s)
    super_control_http_test.go:891: SSE response did not terminate after hub close
```

## Root cause

`ControlEventHub.Close` closed SSE subscribers, which stopped the renderer, but
`ControlHTTPHandler.handleEvents` waited only for `r.Context().Done()`. The
active HTTP response therefore remained open after a hub close. Edge B's
`ControlHTTPClient.Sync` only enables polling after its SSE reader returns an
error; it never observed one and retained peer 101. Instrumenting the original
predicate showed `snapshot_removed=true peer_removed=false`, ruling out a
stale authoritative snapshot/ETag response and a short convergence window.

The fix selects on the renderer subscriber's `Done` channel as well as request
cancellation, allowing the SSE response to end and the existing polling
fallback to apply the deletion. The test harness also gives each already-closed
edge its own existing three-second lifecycle check rather than reusing the
shutdown operation's expired deadline; this does not alter the deleted-peer
three-second convergence contract.

## Verification after the fix

```text
go test -race -count=1 . -run 'TestControlHTTPV2SSETerminates_whenHubCloses'
ok   github.com/KusakabeSi/EtherGuard-VPN  1.072s

go test -race -count=20 . -run 'TestHTTPOnlySuperEndToEnd'
ok   github.com/KusakabeSi/EtherGuard-VPN  19.254s
```
